// A debugger's view of generic procedures (bin/run-tests runs it under gdb
// with generics.gdb and under cdb with generics.cdb). Each instance is a
// procedure of its own (spec 7.11), named as the instance is, pick(T =
// int), in a backtrace too, with its instance's parameter types: an int's
// value, a Vec2 by its address. A line of pick's body has code in each
// instance: gdb sets a breakpoint on it in each ("2 locations") and stops in
// each in turn; cdb refuses it, as a deferred line's ("Ambiguous symbol
// error", spec 10.7), which its script pins on purpose, then sets one on
// each instance, `bm generics!pick*`, at its first instruction, and steps to
// the line. gdb's `break 'pick(T = Vec2)'` sets one on that instance only:
// the first stop is there, though pick(T = int) runs first, and it's hit
// once; `rbreak ^pick(` sets one on each, as `break pick` does once gdb has
// read the symbols. ring's instance takes no N or T: only its result's
// address and the context.
printf :: (format: *u8, ..) -> s32 #foreign libc;

Vec2 :: struct { x: int; y: int; }

pick :: (a: $T, b: T, first: bool) -> T {
    chosen := b;
    if first { chosen = a; }
    return chosen;
}

ring :: ($N: int, $T: Type) -> [N]T {
    r: [N]T;
    return r;
}

main :: () {
    i := pick(3, 4, true);
    v := pick(Vec2.{1, 2}, Vec2.{3, 4}, false);
    r := ring(2, int);
    printf("%lld %lld %lld %lld\n", i, v.x, v.y, r.count);
}
